  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 303 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure of 1035 mb will slide east this \nmorning, keeping the region dry and leading to a cold start to \nthe morning as temperatures hover in the mid 20s to lower 30s \nover the next several hours. Winds this morning will be south-\nsouthwesterly, drawing milder air into the region and helping to\nrebound our temperatures into the mid and upper 50s this \nafternoon. Some light breezes around 10-15 mph in the wider \nvalleys will be possible today as well. Tonight through midweek,\nlows will likely fall into the chilly mid 20s to lower 40s, \nmainly too dry to see much valley fog. Southwest flow will \ncontinue to advect some warmer air in on Monday as highs reach \ntheir peak for the week in the low to mid 60s under sunny skies.\nLarge scale upper level ridging upstream Tuesday and Wednesday \nwill bring us seasonable highs in the upper 50s to lower 60s and\nmainly sunny skies as low humidity and light wind dominates the\nperiod. Through midweek, there is high confidence of no \nprecipitation areawide.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure both aloft and at the surface wl dominate \nour weather this week into next weekend. Latest 00z guidance is a \nlittle more aggressive with cold air advection on Thurs into Friday, \nassociated with northerly flow on front side of redeveloping high \npres from central Canada. The 00z ECMWF shows progged 850mb temps \nbtwn -5C and -7C advecting into our cwa late Thurs into Friday, \nwhile ridge axis is re-established over the western Great Lakes. \nThis results in deep closed off cyclonic circulation over eastern \nNew England, which combined with some weak instability/upslope flow \nmay result in a few sprinkles/mtn flurries late this upcoming week. \nLatest AI runs of the GFS/ECMWF are less aggressive with development \nof late week trof and associated caa, supporting what is advertised \nby the WPC forecast. Highs generally in the upper 40s to mid 50s \nwith lows upper 20s to near 40F daily.",
